    ($SATQ$cc7ffe02$SATQ$, $SATQ$a44626e2-3239-48f1-90f6-145e3b8b2c54$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f95eb4aa-f910-4683-b650-480c2996d699$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Keenan made <math alttext="32"><mn>32</mn>
</math> cups of vegetable broth. Keenan then filled <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> small jars and <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> large jars with all the vegetable broth he made. The equation&nbsp;<math alttext="3 x plus 5 y equals 32"><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></math> represents this situation. Which is the best interpretation of <math alttext="5 y"><mrow>
	<mn>5</mn>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math> in this context?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"17647838-3647-4623-b3e9-1d722518efe3","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The number of large jars Keenan filled</p>"},{"id":"3d1d9788-235c-4a9b-b842-5d7394a33415","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The number of small jars Keenan filled</p>"},{"id":"aabb4df7-af68-4ec5-85ca-330d0070a965","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The total number of cups of vegetable broth in the large jars</p>"},{"id":"266b5f14-5af2-4047-924f-2334f1651374","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The total number of cups of vegetable broth in the small jars</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["aabb4df7-af68-4ec5-85ca-330d0070a965"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the equation <math alttext="3 x plus 5 y equals 32"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>3</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mrow>
			<mn>5</mn>
			<mi>y</mi>
		</mrow>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>32</mn>
</mrow>
</math> represents the situation where Keenan filled <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> small jars and <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> large jars with all the vegetable broth he made, which was <math alttext="32"><mn>32</mn>
</math> cups. Therefore, <math alttext="3 x"><mrow>
	<mn>3</mn>
	<mi>x</mi>
</mrow>
</math> represents the total number of cups of vegetable broth in the small jars and <math alttext="5 y"><mrow>
	<mn>5</mn>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math> represents the total number of cups of vegetable broth in the large jars.&nbsp;</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect. The number of large jars Keenan filled is represented by <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math>, not <math alttext="5 y"><mrow>
	<mn>5</mn>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math>.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect. The number of small jars Keenan filled is represented by <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math>, not <math alttext="5 y"><mrow>
	<mn>5</mn>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math>.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect. The total number of cups of vegetable broth in the small jars is represented by <math alttext="3 x"><mrow>
	<mn>3</mn>
	<mi>x</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, not <math alttext="5 y"><mrow>
	<mn>5</mn>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math>.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"cc7ffe02","external_id":"a44626e2-3239-48f1-90f6-145e3b8b2c54","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"f95eb4aa-f910-4683-b650-480c2996d699","uId":"60124e4f-8d00-466a-ad8f-326993627a9d","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.C.","skill_desc":"Linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Keenan made <math alttext=\"32\"><mn>32</mn>\n</math> cups of vegetable broth. Keenan then filled <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> small jars and <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> large jars with all the vegetable broth he made. The equation&nbsp;<math alttext=\"3 x plus 5 y equals 32\"><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></math> represents this situation. Which is the best interpretation of <math alttext=\"5 y\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> in this context?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"17647838-3647-4623-b3e9-1d722518efe3","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The number of large jars Keenan filled</p>"},{"id":"3d1d9788-235c-4a9b-b842-5d7394a33415","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The number of small jars Keenan filled</p>"},{"id":"aabb4df7-af68-4ec5-85ca-330d0070a965","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The total number of cups of vegetable broth in the large jars</p>"},{"id":"266b5f14-5af2-4047-924f-2334f1651374","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The total number of cups of vegetable broth in the small jars</p>"}],"keys":["aabb4df7-af68-4ec5-85ca-330d0070a965"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the equation <math alttext=\"3 x plus 5 y equals 32\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>